WEBVTT

00:01.200 --> 00:04.920
Every time you ask an AI a question, something happens inside it that nobody fully

00:04.920 --> 00:06.277
understands.

00:06.727 --> 00:10.647
Inside, it's a bit like a galaxy: millions of tiny lights,

00:10.647 --> 00:16.667
flickering on and off, passing signals to each other along threads of light.

00:16.667 --> 00:17.934
Each light is a neuron.

00:18.384 --> 00:22.084
For years, many of the people who study these networks believed they were knotted.

00:22.084 --> 00:27.224
Not arranged into meaningful patterns, like constellations,

00:27.224 --> 00:32.644
but tangled. Ideas with nothing in common, crammed into the same lights,

00:32.644 --> 00:36.772
simply because they fit. There wasn't room to give every idea its own light.

00:37.222 --> 00:40.262
And if that were true, there'd be only one way to understand an AI:

00:40.262 --> 00:44.530
tear the knot apart first, and study the pieces.

00:44.980 --> 00:49.460
The researcher Nick Cammarata spent most of a decade on this question.

00:49.460 --> 00:52.320
In a recent essay, Nick argues that the field got it wrong.

00:52.320 --> 00:57.320
It was never a knot. Researchers just didn't know how to read the constellations

00:57.320 --> 00:58.120
yet.

00:58.394 --> 01:01.571
This video walks through that essay, and how Nick came to think so.

01:02.571 --> 01:07.251
It comes in four parts. First, the story everyone believed.

01:07.251 --> 01:11.811
Then we reopen the evidence, case by case. Then,

01:11.811 --> 01:16.714
how networks really work. And finally, what it all means.

01:20.714 --> 01:26.074
Part one: the story everyone believed. Let's zoom all the way in to a single light

01:26.074 --> 01:30.474
from that galaxy, and picture it as a little building in a town.

01:30.474 --> 01:34.834
So, what is a neuron in an AI? It's simpler than it sounds.

01:34.834 --> 01:37.974
A neuron is a tiny detector. It looks at what's coming in,

01:37.974 --> 01:40.354
and if it sees something it cares about, it lights up.

01:40.354 --> 01:45.736
We'll start with AIs that look at pictures, because pictures are easy to show.

01:46.186 --> 01:48.966
So you can find out what a neuron cares about by showing it a lot of pictures,

01:48.966 --> 01:51.706
and keeping the ones that make it light up brightest.

01:51.706 --> 01:57.506
Those pictures end up in its windows. A neuron's windows show what lights it up

01:57.506 --> 01:58.306
most.

01:58.333 --> 02:00.313
An image network is built in layers, like districts.

02:00.313 --> 02:03.513
Near the coast, the edge of the city where the pixels come in,

02:03.513 --> 02:08.413
neurons detect simple things: edges, curves, colors.

02:08.413 --> 02:13.353
Further inland, textures and eyes and wheels. And on the horizon,

02:13.353 --> 02:18.404
in the last district, whole ideas: dogs, cars, ladybugs.

02:18.854 --> 02:21.594
Neurons pass signals to each other along roads,

02:21.594 --> 02:25.434
and a chain of neurons working together is called a circuit.

02:25.434 --> 02:29.174
If you can read the neurons and the circuits, you can start to understand what the

02:29.174 --> 02:30.736
network is actually doing.

02:31.186 --> 02:36.026
That was the hope of a field called mechanistic interpretability.

02:36.026 --> 02:40.926
Look inside. Read the parts. Understand the machine.

02:40.926 --> 02:44.563
A network whose parts you can read like that is what Nick calls clean.

02:47.963 --> 02:51.431
But there was a problem. Some neurons didn't seem to make any sense.

02:51.881 --> 02:57.121
The most famous one was in an older image model called InceptionV1.

02:57.121 --> 03:00.141
It lit up for cat faces. And for the fronts of cars.

03:00.141 --> 03:06.510
Cats and cars. What do those have in common?

03:06.960 --> 03:12.260
Researchers called these neurons polysemantic: one neuron,

03:12.260 --> 03:17.160
many unrelated meanings. And there was worse. In the last district of that same

03:17.160 --> 03:19.810
network, almost nothing made sense at all.

03:20.260 --> 03:23.900
The leading explanation was called superposition.

03:23.900 --> 03:28.400
Here's the idea. Imagine a city with far more people than houses.

03:28.400 --> 03:32.760
The houses are neurons. The people are ideas the network needs to keep track of.

03:32.760 --> 03:34.520
You could give similar people a house together,

03:34.520 --> 03:39.840
a family. Or you could do something stranger: put total strangers in the same house,

03:39.840 --> 03:41.496
as long as they're never home at the same time.

03:41.946 --> 03:44.526
Strangers rarely bump into each other, so it mostly works.

03:44.526 --> 03:48.546
And if a network were short on neurons, superposition said,

03:48.546 --> 03:52.686
that's exactly what it would do: pack unrelated things together,

03:52.686 --> 03:54.783
because they rarely collide.

03:55.233 --> 03:59.533
That would be terrible news. Because if neurons are packed with strangers,

03:59.533 --> 04:02.844
then what shares a neuron tells you nothing. The city would be a knot.

04:03.294 --> 04:04.794
So the field built a new kind of tool to unfold it:

04:04.794 --> 04:10.494
a second model, called a sparse autoencoder, trained to take the network apart and

04:10.494 --> 04:15.014
rebuild it as a long list of cleaner pieces, which you'd study instead of the

04:15.014 --> 04:17.790
original. Think of it as an Unfolding Machine.

04:18.240 --> 04:22.860
And Nick was in the thick of it, helping make the case for superposition.

04:22.860 --> 04:25.040
Nick was one of the people who first proposed it,

04:25.040 --> 04:27.233
and believed it for years.

04:34.633 --> 04:39.373
Part two: the evidence. That was the story everyone believed:

04:39.373 --> 04:43.493
neurons packed with strangers, and a machine to pull them apart.

04:43.493 --> 04:46.853
But the knot story rested on a few famous cases.

04:46.853 --> 04:49.332
So let's reopen them, one at a time.

04:50.132 --> 04:54.312
Years later, after many months studying neurons in modern image and language models,

04:54.312 --> 04:58.992
and finding them surprisingly clean, Nick went back to the original crime scenes:

04:58.992 --> 05:03.618
the famous cases the knot story was built on. Starting with the cat and car neuron.

05:04.068 --> 05:06.168
Nick had looked at its pictures hundreds of times.

05:06.168 --> 05:12.668
This time, Nick tried something new: spending a few seconds asking,

05:12.668 --> 05:14.787
what one thing could this be?

05:15.237 --> 05:18.097
And then it was obvious. It isn't a cat and car neuron.

05:18.097 --> 05:19.200
It's a V neuron.

05:19.650 --> 05:23.430
Pointed things make it light up. Look at the roads leading into it,

05:23.430 --> 05:26.230
from the neurons that feed it: they come from a flame,

05:26.230 --> 05:29.630
the jets of water from a fireboat, and fox faces.

05:29.630 --> 05:31.875
All V-shaped.

05:32.325 --> 05:36.045
And look at what switches it off. Round things.

05:36.045 --> 05:41.725
A tennis ball. A clock. A snail shell. It isn't the opposite of the V.

05:41.725 --> 05:43.903
It's what the V is being told apart from.

05:44.353 --> 05:48.213
Bullet train noses. The triangle of balls on a pool table.

05:48.213 --> 05:51.713
It fires for those too. It was never about cats or cars.

05:51.713 --> 05:52.513
It's a shape.

05:52.750 --> 05:58.390
Here's a useful trick from the essay: try explaining a neuron without any nouns.

05:58.390 --> 06:04.570
People, and AI assistants, love describing neurons as lists of nouns:

06:04.570 --> 06:08.870
cats, cars. But a neuron is often better described as a verb.

06:08.870 --> 06:12.830
It's not naming what it sees. It's making a distinction:

06:12.830 --> 06:17.483
pointed, not round. And passing that on to the neurons further down the road.

06:17.933 --> 06:22.193
Why didn't anyone see it before? The neuron's portrait was painted by a technique

06:22.193 --> 06:26.913
called feature visualization. Think of it as a Sketch Artist:

06:26.913 --> 06:28.753
it paints the picture a neuron would like best.

06:28.753 --> 06:34.753
And this portrait had cat whiskers in it. That's because the artist follows the

06:34.753 --> 06:38.213
easiest route to make the neuron light up, and once it finds the cat route,

06:38.213 --> 06:42.612
it paints everything that route brings, whiskers included.

06:43.062 --> 06:47.442
So the first lesson: a neuron that seems to mean two or three things is often one

06:47.442 --> 06:51.282
thing we haven't found the word for yet, or a small family of related ones.

06:51.282 --> 06:55.048
The grouping isn't noise. It's a clue.

06:59.048 --> 07:03.368
The second crime scene was the original superposition circuit itself.

07:03.368 --> 07:08.548
Nick found it in 2019, and with Chris Olah read it as superposition.

07:08.548 --> 07:11.566
It became the example the paper Zoom In used to introduce the idea.

07:12.016 --> 07:14.576
It looked like a car signal was being hidden inside a group of dog neurons,

07:14.576 --> 07:18.916
as if the dogs were a secret storage locker, where the car could be stored and

07:18.916 --> 07:25.068
picked up later. Strangers sharing a house: exactly what superposition predicts.

07:25.518 --> 07:27.018
But look at how Nick and Chris saw those neurons:

07:27.018 --> 07:31.818
through the Sketch Artist, the same tool that painted the cat whiskers.

07:31.818 --> 07:34.878
And deep in this network, the artist gets lazy.

07:34.878 --> 07:38.284
There are lots of dog neurons, so the easiest painting is usually a dog.

07:38.734 --> 07:42.694
Nudge the artist even slightly toward the routes that real pictures take,

07:42.694 --> 07:44.354
so it paints things that look like real photos,

07:44.354 --> 07:50.911
and the portraits change. Two of those "dog" neurons are actually car neurons.

07:51.361 --> 07:53.041
And the other two respond to cars and dogs alike,

07:53.041 --> 07:56.461
for one shared reason: which way they're facing.

07:56.461 --> 07:58.670
They're pose neurons.

07:59.120 --> 08:01.760
It turns out the network has more than a hundred of these pose neurons,

08:01.760 --> 08:06.780
each one clean. And nothing was hiding. The car signal wasn't stored in the dogs.

08:06.780 --> 08:10.998
It kept going, splitting into more and more specific car neurons.

08:11.448 --> 08:14.028
You might think: that's only because the model was small.

08:14.028 --> 08:19.148
A bigger model would separate dogs and cars. But DINOv2,

08:19.148 --> 08:22.328
more than ten times larger and about ten years newer,

08:22.328 --> 08:29.628
still has generalized pose neurons. Every vision model Nick has looked at does.

08:29.628 --> 08:37.008
So the field's main example of superposition was really ordinary generalization,

08:37.008 --> 08:38.609
seen through a lazy painting.

08:42.609 --> 08:45.509
Both cases came down to the tools. And the story of this field,

08:45.509 --> 08:49.689
as Nick tells it, is mostly a story of discovering that what was under the

08:49.689 --> 08:55.024
microscope was better than anyone thought. The microscope just wasn't tuned right.

08:55.474 --> 08:58.674
The first portraits of neurons, the Sketch Artist's early paintings,

08:58.674 --> 09:04.934
were noisy, psychedelic smears. Then researchers added one ingredient,

09:04.934 --> 09:09.294
called transformation robustness, which means the picture still has to work when

09:09.294 --> 09:12.674
it's nudged or turned a little, and the same neurons came out crisp.

09:12.674 --> 09:15.541
Later, a few small tweaks let researchers see much deeper.

09:15.991 --> 09:19.311
But the worst place of all was the third case: the last district of InceptionV1,

09:19.311 --> 09:24.731
the cat and car network. Here, almost nothing looked interpretable.

09:24.731 --> 09:30.971
One researcher, Liv Gorton, said there were essentially no interpretable neurons

09:30.971 --> 09:34.417
there at all. Nick would have guessed maybe five percent.

09:34.867 --> 09:37.787
And the leading explanation was the knot again:

09:37.787 --> 09:42.591
too many ideas, too few neurons, so they got crammed in at random.

09:43.041 --> 09:45.561
But here's the thing. Take a more modern network design,

09:45.561 --> 09:49.021
called a ConvNeXt, with the same number of parameters,

09:49.021 --> 09:51.481
so the same size, and trained on the same pictures.

09:51.481 --> 09:54.381
The only real differences are engineering upgrades,

09:54.381 --> 10:00.081
like residual connections and layer normalization.

10:00.081 --> 10:01.638
And the last district is beautiful.

10:02.088 --> 10:06.888
Same size, same data. Better tools alone took the old network from about five

10:06.888 --> 10:11.768
percent to about fifty. Retraining it with modern settings got it to about seventy.

10:11.768 --> 10:17.328
And in the new design, ninety-nine to a hundred percent of its last-layer neurons

10:17.328 --> 10:18.725
are easy to interpret.

10:19.175 --> 10:22.495
The old network wasn't packing strangers together.

10:22.495 --> 10:26.395
It was a district built badly, seen through a dirty lens.

10:26.395 --> 10:30.655
Upgrades made by people who had no interest in interpretability at all made it

10:30.655 --> 10:31.455
readable.

10:35.408 --> 10:39.068
But even that messy last district of the old network wasn't random.

10:39.068 --> 10:41.225
To see that, you have to watch a neuron grow.

10:41.675 --> 10:45.975
Nick retrained the old network from scratch and watched one neuron form.

10:45.975 --> 10:48.875
It started out lighting up on fields of yellow flowers,

10:48.875 --> 10:50.764
in photos that sometimes had a dog in them.

10:51.214 --> 10:54.134
Then it grew along each part of those photos. Toward other green,

10:54.134 --> 10:57.714
round things, like tennis balls and fruit. Toward dogs,

10:57.714 --> 11:02.566
without any flowers. Then into broccoli, until the flowers were nearly gone.

11:03.016 --> 11:07.616
In the modern network, the same kind of neuron reached for the balls and the fruit

11:07.616 --> 11:10.896
early on, then let them go, and stayed on the fields.

11:10.896 --> 11:15.716
The better design kept it focused. The old one let it wander,

11:15.716 --> 11:19.305
but every step of the wandering made sense.

11:19.755 --> 11:25.055
Nick calls this recursive generalization. An earlier paper Nick worked on,

11:25.055 --> 11:31.275
Multimodal Neurons, first spotted it. A neuron generalizes along one direction,

11:31.275 --> 11:35.275
then sprouts off in others, each new branch close to the last.

11:35.275 --> 11:38.245
Every branch makes sense on its own. You just have to follow it.

11:38.695 --> 11:43.915
It happens in language models too. One neuron fired on the letters "iy",

11:43.915 --> 11:49.795
in words like Miyazaki. Then on "diy". Then on "do it yourself",

11:49.795 --> 11:54.635
which doesn't contain "iy" at all. It followed the meaning,

11:54.635 --> 11:56.234
not the letters.

11:56.684 --> 12:01.924
A geology neuron grew a branch for Cesium, a coding library for maps of the earth.

12:01.924 --> 12:07.244
Some are obvious: an enemy neuron fired for a Russian poem about enemies.

12:07.244 --> 12:12.690
Some take digging. But so far, almost none are random.

12:16.490 --> 12:18.970
So now there were two explanations for neurons with mixed meanings,

12:18.970 --> 12:21.349
and they predict opposite things.

12:21.799 --> 12:26.019
Generalization says neighbors share, neighbors in meaning,

12:26.019 --> 12:30.059
like the family sharing a house. A neuron for floppy-eared dogs is really a

12:30.059 --> 12:36.739
collection of slightly different floppy ears. Superposition says strangers share,

12:36.739 --> 12:41.774
like strangers on different shifts: a neuron for floppy-eared dogs and Latin poetry.

12:42.224 --> 12:44.884
Here's what that looks like with thirty-two things and a handful of neurons.

12:44.884 --> 12:50.424
Generalization groups similar things, and with fewer neurons the groups just get

12:50.424 --> 12:53.579
coarser. Superposition scatters them.

12:54.029 --> 12:57.609
And this is testable. You can watch a neuron grow and see which one happens.

12:57.609 --> 13:02.443
Or you can squeeze the network and see what it does under pressure.

13:02.893 --> 13:04.693
So Nick took a layer famous for its curve detectors,

13:04.693 --> 13:08.093
cut it from four hundred and eighty neurons down to ten,

13:08.093 --> 13:10.953
with no way around, and retrained it from scratch.

13:10.953 --> 13:15.793
If squeezing forced superposition, this should be dramatic:

13:15.793 --> 13:19.911
curves, spirals and spider webs, crammed together.

13:20.361 --> 13:25.121
Instead, the neurons stayed clean. Just coarser.

13:25.121 --> 13:30.141
One "curves and loops" neuron instead of ten curve detectors.

13:30.141 --> 13:34.261
Two circle neurons instead of many. And when the Unfolding Machine,

13:34.261 --> 13:37.001
the tool built to pull the knot apart, was sent in to look for hidden curve

13:37.001 --> 13:43.571
detectors, it found none: just broad, bunched-up versions of the same shapes.

13:44.021 --> 13:45.941
Nick did the same to the last district of a modern network:

13:45.941 --> 13:49.741
a thousand and twenty-four neurons down to thirteen.

13:49.741 --> 13:55.721
Same thing. Coarser, not scrambled. In these tests,

13:55.721 --> 13:59.781
under pressure, a network doesn't switch to packing.

13:59.781 --> 14:02.422
It just paints with a broader brush.

14:03.822 --> 14:07.542
Size works the same way. Nick trained versions of a network roughly three hundred

14:07.542 --> 14:11.282
times apart in size. The smallest has one broad car neuron,

14:11.282 --> 14:15.582
for cars, trains and carts. The biggest splits that into specialists:

14:15.582 --> 14:20.462
taxis, ambulances, convertibles, even different views of a taxi.

14:20.462 --> 14:24.378
Less room makes neurons broader, not scrambled.

14:24.828 --> 14:26.748
And when Nick ran the Unfolding Machine on the last district,

14:26.748 --> 14:32.308
it mostly found near-copies of single neurons: about eighty-eight percent of its

14:32.308 --> 14:36.468
activity. It did help with "what's in this picture?".

14:36.468 --> 14:39.788
But where the neurons were building blocks, like spider legs and webs,

14:39.788 --> 14:45.068
the machine's pieces were those same blocks regrouped by setting:

14:45.068 --> 14:50.108
spiders in different places. The tool built to unfold the knot mostly kept finding

14:50.108 --> 14:51.486
the neurons that were already there.

14:55.486 --> 14:58.986
So the image cases came out clean. But language was where Nick,

14:58.986 --> 15:03.626
then a true believer in superposition, expected the knot to be worst.

15:03.626 --> 15:09.451
Studying language model neurons, Nick kept expecting to hit it any day.

15:09.901 --> 15:11.701
Nick found one that fired on the letters "P R",

15:11.701 --> 15:17.741
and on strange strings of Base64, a way of writing computer data as text,

15:17.741 --> 15:24.661
and on Bengali text. Finally, it seemed, an arbitrary mixture!

15:24.661 --> 15:29.341
Then Nick decoded the code: words with "P R". And the Bengali,

15:29.341 --> 15:33.561
translated: "P R" again. It was one idea all along,

15:33.561 --> 15:36.670
the letters P R, in whatever form they showed up.

15:37.120 --> 15:41.200
A coworker found a neuron that seemed to fire on random commas.

15:41.200 --> 15:46.300
It turned out to track Colorado. Models use spots like commas to jot down a running

15:46.300 --> 15:50.620
summary of what's been said so far, and Colorado was in that summary.

15:50.620 --> 15:54.038
Turn the neuron up by hand, and the model starts planning a hike.

15:54.638 --> 15:58.278
Case after case, the mess resolved. And each time,

15:58.278 --> 16:02.398
superposition just moved, like the old "god of the gaps" idea,

16:02.398 --> 16:07.578
where whatever science can't explain yet gets credited to God.

16:07.578 --> 16:12.238
Whatever couldn't be explained yet got credited to superposition.

16:12.238 --> 16:15.978
If the strong signals were clean, it was hiding in the weak ones.

16:15.978 --> 16:20.280
Or between neurons. Or in the neurons nobody had checked yet.

16:20.730 --> 16:23.890
Nick now thinks superposition was being treated as the default,

16:23.890 --> 16:26.370
by Nick and much of the field, when it should be the last resort.

16:26.370 --> 16:31.130
It's the most radical explanation, and the hardest to test.

16:31.130 --> 16:34.070
Its only symptom is that something doesn't make sense.

16:34.070 --> 16:36.470
But that's also exactly what a bad tool, a bad design,

16:36.470 --> 16:38.473
or a branch you haven't followed yet looks like.

16:38.923 --> 16:43.603
An Anthropic paper from 2022 noted that superposition is often treated as the

16:43.603 --> 16:49.323
default because it feels intuitive, while there's little direct evidence for it.

16:49.323 --> 16:53.263
To Nick's knowledge, no one has found a real superposition circuit since the one

16:53.263 --> 16:57.923
Nick found in 2019: the hidden car circuit, where a car signal seemed to hide among

16:57.923 --> 17:00.563
dog neurons. And that one was a mistake.

17:08.563 --> 17:14.223
Part three. Every case ended the same way: the mess was a clue no one could read

17:14.223 --> 17:18.343
yet. A blurry tool, an older design, or a branch nobody had followed.

17:18.343 --> 17:24.450
Never strangers packed together. So how do networks actually work?

17:25.250 --> 17:29.610
Once you stop expecting knots, neurons start to look less like labels and more like

17:29.610 --> 17:30.662
traffic control.

17:31.112 --> 17:35.492
Early on, cars and dogs travel together, because they share shapes and poses.

17:35.492 --> 17:39.152
Then a neuron acts as a gate. It lights up for cars and switches off for dogs,

17:39.152 --> 17:43.812
sending each into its own neighborhood. Later neurons split them finer:

17:43.812 --> 17:45.786
taxis from ambulances.

17:46.236 --> 17:49.436
Follow one sports car through the last districts of a big model.

17:49.436 --> 17:53.336
Partway in, it's grouped with other boxy things,

17:53.336 --> 17:57.256
tables and cabinets, and kept apart from snakes and dogs.

17:57.256 --> 18:00.908
By the end, the question is whether it's a convertible or a station wagon.

18:01.358 --> 18:05.998
Every neuron decides by vote. Each incoming road casts a vote for "light up" or

18:05.998 --> 18:10.738
"stay dark", and the votes add up. That's why the things that switch a neuron off,

18:10.738 --> 18:14.578
its negative side, matter almost as much as the things that light it up,

18:14.578 --> 18:19.118
its windows. Like the V neuron, pointed versus round,

18:19.118 --> 18:22.873
they tell you which distinction is being made right here.

18:23.323 --> 18:26.623
In the last district, the thing that most switches a neuron off is usually its

18:26.623 --> 18:32.143
near-twin, its closest look-alike: the thing it most needs to tell itself apart

18:32.143 --> 18:37.023
from. Taxis versus ambulances. Broccoli versus cauliflower.

18:37.023 --> 18:40.717
Spoonbills versus flamingos.

18:41.317 --> 18:46.017
Remember where superposition retreated to: the weak signals.

18:46.017 --> 18:49.577
So what about those faint signals, the whispers?

18:49.577 --> 18:53.557
Run a hundred million words past one neuron in a language model,

18:53.557 --> 18:57.837
and almost all of them barely register. Only about a hundred and sixty reach even

18:57.837 --> 18:58.807
half its peak.

18:59.257 --> 19:03.237
You can silence almost all the whispers across a whole network and barely change

19:03.237 --> 19:09.457
what it does. What you lose are tiny tie-breakers between options it was already

19:09.457 --> 19:10.653
torn between.

19:11.103 --> 19:15.163
It also helps to think in pages. A neuron that fires on one word in a hundred sounds

19:15.163 --> 19:21.503
uncommon. But that's about four times a page: far too often for a neuron about a

19:21.503 --> 19:27.763
narrow idea, like a rare feeling. The really specific ones fire every few dozen

19:27.763 --> 19:32.823
pages. So if a label sounds narrow but the neuron fires on every page,

19:32.823 --> 19:35.076
the label is probably wrong.

19:35.526 --> 19:37.086
And the whispers that remain usually make sense.

19:37.086 --> 19:40.906
A Titanic neuron fires a little for other ocean voyages.

19:40.906 --> 19:46.706
Neurons are analogy machines: something can be a bit Titanic-ish.

19:46.706 --> 19:47.906
That's what most whispers are.

19:51.906 --> 19:53.786
We've already met a few language neurons along the way:

19:53.786 --> 19:59.466
the letters P R, and Colorado. But our closest looks have been inside image

19:59.466 --> 20:05.526
networks, because pictures are easy to show. And language models are the AI most

20:05.526 --> 20:10.026
people actually use: the kind you chat with. Does the whole story hold there?

20:10.026 --> 20:14.440
Let's take a proper look inside one, and ask three questions.

20:17.040 --> 20:19.420
Question one: how many neurons can we actually read?

20:19.420 --> 20:25.460
In 2023, studies of small language models rated almost none of their neurons as

20:25.460 --> 20:31.320
understandable. The project Nick started at OpenAI found under two percent.

20:31.320 --> 20:35.178
Today it's different. In early audits of a modern model,

20:35.178 --> 20:41.360
Qwen3-32B, Nick's team estimates about ninety percent of neurons have one main job.

20:41.360 --> 20:47.127
Seven percent have two related jobs. Only two to four percent aren't understood yet.

20:47.577 --> 20:50.457
Question two: can we follow a model's reasoning,

20:50.457 --> 20:54.617
one step at a time? Here's an example first traced by Anthropic:

20:54.617 --> 20:59.317
a doctor's question about a pregnant patient. Inside the model,

20:59.317 --> 21:04.017
a courier service called attention carries notes between words,

21:04.017 --> 21:05.977
so a neuron in one place can know what was said in another.

21:05.977 --> 21:10.537
In this medical example, the couriers gather the facts of the case,

21:10.537 --> 21:15.360
like the headache, onto one place, where the diagnosis happens.

21:15.810 --> 21:18.190
And Nick's team can follow what happens there in plain neurons.

21:18.190 --> 21:22.530
Given all the symptoms of a pregnant patient, a small group of four neurons

21:22.530 --> 21:29.090
concludes: preeclampsia, a dangerous blood pressure condition in pregnancy.

21:29.090 --> 21:32.730
Switch them off, and the model starts asking about itching instead,

21:32.730 --> 21:35.866
the sign of a different condition, as if it moved on to its next guess.

21:36.316 --> 21:40.216
It seems to work in giant models too. In a model with trillions of parameters,

21:40.216 --> 21:43.856
Nick's team traced its answer to "I found a wallet,

21:43.856 --> 21:46.596
what should I do?" The facts are a wallet, cash,

21:46.596 --> 21:50.636
an ID. They light up neurons for lost property and honesty,

21:50.636 --> 21:56.078
and the answer comes out: return it. Full audits at that scale haven't started yet.

21:56.878 --> 22:01.138
Question three: what about the mess that's left?

22:01.138 --> 22:06.918
Some of it turned out to be plumbing. Modern neurons multiply two signals together.

22:06.918 --> 22:09.898
And just like in school maths, when both are negative,

22:09.898 --> 22:13.638
the result comes out positive, so a neuron can light up for the opposite of its real

22:13.638 --> 22:19.638
job. From the outside, that looks like noise. Separate those two cases,

22:19.638 --> 22:21.419
and the noise sorts itself out.

22:21.869 --> 22:24.789
And there's a river that runs through every district,

22:24.789 --> 22:29.409
carrying information forward: the residual stream.

22:29.409 --> 22:32.169
Every building can read from it and write to it.

22:32.169 --> 22:35.889
Researchers worried it might be a tangle too. So far it has been readable.

22:35.889 --> 22:40.249
And when Nick's team squeezed it narrower in an image network,

22:40.249 --> 22:43.924
it got messier, but nothing looked knotted.

22:44.374 --> 22:48.034
Finally, even famous examples of confusion look different now.

22:48.034 --> 22:53.514
One neuron from an early Anthropic paper, described as a mix of academic citations,

22:53.514 --> 22:57.354
dialogue, web requests and Korean text, looks like a neuron whose job may be to stop

22:57.354 --> 23:00.034
the model from writing a period. Remove one like it,

23:00.034 --> 23:04.274
and periods come sooner. So language models tell the same story:

23:04.274 --> 23:08.174
most neurons are readable, their reasoning can be followed,

23:08.174 --> 23:11.542
and the leftover mess has ordinary causes.

23:15.542 --> 23:18.422
A fair worry: maybe these are all just made-up stories.

23:18.422 --> 23:23.642
Any neuron can get a vague label, like "a multi-purpose language neuron".

23:23.642 --> 23:28.471
So researchers use tests that an explanation can fail.

23:28.921 --> 23:32.841
One test: an AI agent, an AI that runs experiments by itself,

23:32.841 --> 23:37.141
studies a neuron and writes a short explanation.

23:37.141 --> 23:41.061
A second agent sees only that explanation, never the neuron,

23:41.061 --> 23:44.339
and writes a sentence to make it fire as hard as possible.

23:44.789 --> 23:49.329
Sometimes that one sentence makes the neuron fire harder than anything found in ten

23:49.329 --> 23:55.126
billion words of real text. Often, the explanation hit the center of the target.

23:55.576 --> 23:58.116
Agents also wrote examples that should fire a neuron,

23:58.116 --> 24:04.116
near-misses that shouldn't, and the most distant idea they could imagine.

24:04.116 --> 24:09.556
The examples fired the neuron strongly three quarters of the time.

24:09.556 --> 24:13.156
Near-misses, eleven percent. The distant ideas,

24:13.156 --> 24:17.336
about one percent. That's what a real explanation looks like:

24:17.336 --> 24:20.263
it hits where it should, and misses where it should.

24:21.663 --> 24:25.583
General scores only go so far, though. A wrong explanation,

24:25.583 --> 24:27.883
like the "cats and cars" label the V neuron once had,

24:27.883 --> 24:31.743
can pass a lot of tests. Science works like this everywhere:

24:31.743 --> 24:37.763
Newton's gravity explained almost everything, then broke on a tiny wobble in

24:37.763 --> 24:42.103
Mercury's orbit. That wobble was a specific test.

24:42.103 --> 24:44.893
The best tests are specific to the idea you're testing.

24:48.893 --> 24:52.953
So the explanations hold up when they're given a chance to lose.

24:52.953 --> 24:58.373
Which leaves the deepest question: why are networks organized at all?

24:58.373 --> 25:01.239
Why not a knot? Nick has a guess. It's a race.

25:01.689 --> 25:04.969
Here's the idea. A brand new network sees its first photo:

25:04.969 --> 25:11.789
a Labrador on grass. Inside, two possible circuits race to explain it.

25:11.789 --> 25:15.049
Let's draw them as animals. The first is narrow:

25:15.049 --> 25:20.689
it looks for something hyper-specific, this exact pixel being this exact color.

25:20.689 --> 25:24.109
Picture it as a little meerkat. The second is broad:

25:24.109 --> 25:27.989
it looks for green grass and tan fur. Picture it as a big grazer,

25:27.989 --> 25:30.968
made of grass and fur. So, which one wins?

25:31.418 --> 25:33.838
The broad one. It fires on more of the picture,

25:33.838 --> 25:38.558
so it gets more of the learning signal, the feedback that tells a network what to

25:38.558 --> 25:41.818
strengthen. It grows faster, and the narrow one starves.

25:41.818 --> 25:46.698
And when the next photo with grass arrives, the grass circuit already has a head

25:46.698 --> 25:48.138
start.

25:48.738 --> 25:52.338
Repeat that over millions of photos, and you get something like an ecosystem.

25:52.338 --> 25:58.838
A neighborhood that's already good at something captures the next related thing

25:58.838 --> 26:02.358
before anyone new can move in. That, Nick suspects,

26:02.358 --> 26:06.498
is why similar things cluster together. It's the same growth we saw when one neuron

26:06.498 --> 26:10.436
spread from yellow flowers to dogs, tennis balls and broccoli.

26:11.036 --> 26:14.116
The race explains some puzzles too. Take rare things,

26:14.116 --> 26:17.876
which barely ever show up. You might expect them to be hidden away somewhere odd.

26:17.876 --> 26:22.376
Instead, rare things are often gathered into one place:

26:22.376 --> 26:25.221
one triceratops neuron.

26:25.671 --> 26:30.271
And attacks designed to fool the network use those same normal roads.

26:30.271 --> 26:34.551
Make that triceratops skull look like a jaguar to the model,

26:34.551 --> 26:38.551
with a strong attack that has to survive being shifted and turned,

26:38.551 --> 26:41.331
and it paints a cat face and spotted fur onto the skull,

26:41.331 --> 26:45.026
switching on the real jaguar neurons.

26:45.626 --> 26:50.366
Researchers tested this idea by teaching a network something absurd:

26:50.366 --> 26:55.126
that a photo of an umbrella is a bee. It didn't build a secret hidden "umbrella bee"

26:55.126 --> 27:00.366
detector. In just nine training steps, it built an off-ramp from its umbrella road

27:00.366 --> 27:04.308
onto its existing bee road. It reused what it already had.

27:04.758 --> 27:07.958
The famous "Toy Models of Superposition" experiments,

27:07.958 --> 27:11.718
from Anthropic, are careful work, and they do show packing.

27:11.718 --> 27:15.578
But in their main setup, every idea is a stranger to every other.

27:15.578 --> 27:19.358
In real data, almost nothing is related to nothing.

27:19.358 --> 27:22.078
So Nick reads the packing as real, but as a feature of the toy world.

27:22.078 --> 27:25.718
And to be clear: a network can still hold more ideas than it has neurons,

27:25.718 --> 27:31.578
by combining neurons. That's good news. The worry was only ever unrelated things

27:31.578 --> 27:32.843
packed together.

27:40.843 --> 27:46.083
Part four. Networks build clean, readable parts,

27:46.083 --> 27:49.643
and they seem to get there through one simple way of learning:

27:49.643 --> 27:55.363
generalization. The mess was never strangers packed together.

27:55.363 --> 27:58.483
So we may not have to tear the knot apart first.

27:58.483 --> 27:59.467
What does that mean for us?

28:00.267 --> 28:05.467
So here's what "already clean" means. Not that every neuron matches a word we

28:05.467 --> 28:09.967
already have. It means the network's parts are built to be read,

28:09.967 --> 28:15.727
by the network's own downstream neurons, the ones further down the road.

28:15.727 --> 28:18.121
If we learn to read them the same way, we can understand it.

28:18.571 --> 28:23.431
And bigger isn't necessarily harder. Remember the car neurons:

28:23.431 --> 28:27.611
one broad one in the smallest network, specialists in the biggest.

28:27.611 --> 28:31.351
In a small town, there's no financial district:

28:31.351 --> 28:34.691
a bank here, an office there. In New York, you can find it on a map in a minute.

28:34.691 --> 28:39.680
Bigger models have narrower, cleaner neurons that are easier to find.

28:40.130 --> 28:42.390
The danger is forcing our own categories onto it.

28:42.390 --> 28:47.190
If you arrive in Tokyo looking for Brooklyn, and stitch Brooklyn together from

28:47.190 --> 28:52.070
seventy scattered pieces of Tokyo, you'll decide Tokyo is a mess.

28:52.070 --> 28:56.790
That's what happened to the V neuron: researchers went looking for cats and cars,

28:56.790 --> 29:01.870
so that's all they saw. And an Unfolding Machine trained on that layer splits the V

29:01.870 --> 29:04.471
right back into a cat piece and a car piece.

29:04.921 --> 29:09.021
What we need are better maps: views that let you zoom out to whole neighborhoods,

29:09.021 --> 29:13.001
while every street stays connected to the real buildings underneath.

29:13.001 --> 29:16.101
Zoom in on a network's decision for "killer whale",

29:16.101 --> 29:19.081
and it turns out to be three routes: one for whales,

29:19.081 --> 29:24.381
one for sharks and rays, and one for fish held up by proud fishermen.

29:24.381 --> 29:25.998
Each route makes sense on its own.

29:26.448 --> 29:29.668
People worry that something dangerous, like deception,

29:29.668 --> 29:35.228
could hide in some activation we missed. Nick worries about something else.

29:35.228 --> 29:38.168
In every case here, the answer was in plain sight.

29:38.168 --> 29:42.968
We just didn't know how to read it. Deception might hide from someone reading the

29:42.968 --> 29:46.848
model's answers. But it has no reason to hide from the model's own circuits:

29:46.848 --> 29:49.808
they need to read it. If we become readers too,

29:49.808 --> 29:53.908
it should be readable to us. Nick's fear is that it will be right there,

29:53.908 --> 29:56.514
in plain sight, and we won't know how to read it.

29:56.964 --> 30:01.144
And the mistakes aren't symmetric. If you assume a network is clean and you're

30:01.144 --> 30:05.844
wrong, a messy neuron is a lead: you investigate.

30:05.844 --> 30:09.384
If you assume it's a knot and you're wrong, a messy neuron is a dead end:

30:09.384 --> 30:14.202
you stop looking. Nick made that second mistake for years.

30:15.602 --> 30:18.602
If Nick could send a note back eight years, to a younger self,

30:18.602 --> 30:21.562
the essay suggests it would say something like this.

30:21.562 --> 30:25.842
Pay attention to the giant pose circuit right in front of you.

30:25.842 --> 30:29.522
Treat what switches a neuron off as a gate, not an opposite.

30:29.522 --> 30:32.882
Don't trust the Sketch Artist in the last districts.

30:32.882 --> 30:36.562
And whenever something looks like a mess, look harder.

30:36.562 --> 30:40.782
The mess usually points to something you haven't understood yet.

30:41.232 --> 30:46.812
Understanding compounds, like a crossword. At first you're stuck on one clue.

30:46.812 --> 30:49.532
But every answer you fill in constrains the others,

30:49.532 --> 30:52.588
and once the grid starts filling up, it goes faster and faster.

30:56.588 --> 30:59.528
If understanding compounds, the job is to fill in the grid faster.

30:59.528 --> 31:04.508
Here's what that might look like: a laboratory of automated scientists,

31:04.508 --> 31:08.008
AI agents like the ones that wrote those test sentences,

31:08.008 --> 31:11.808
watching a network as it learns, labeling neurons,

31:11.808 --> 31:15.864
finding patterns, testing them, again and again.

31:16.314 --> 31:20.814
The pieces already exist. Capable AI agents were built for other reasons.

31:20.814 --> 31:26.274
Modern architectures were built because they learn well.

31:26.274 --> 31:31.114
Both happen to make interpretability easier. So maybe we just plug one into the

31:31.114 --> 31:31.914
other.

31:31.920 --> 31:37.560
Every neuron can be read. Every experiment can be rerun on an identical copy.

31:37.560 --> 31:40.640
It's the best laboratory in all of natural science.

31:40.640 --> 31:44.420
What we never had were enough scientists. Now we do.

31:45.820 --> 31:49.180
That's been the pattern in AI all along. The simple thing,

31:49.180 --> 31:51.780
done at enormous scale, beats the clever thing.

31:51.780 --> 31:55.880
The AI researcher Rich Sutton called it the bitter lesson.

31:55.880 --> 31:59.620
Maybe interpretability has its own bitter lesson:

31:59.620 --> 32:06.620
scaling ordinary investigation beats inventing ever cleverer ways to take networks

32:06.620 --> 32:07.420
apart.

32:07.610 --> 32:12.950
So, Nick's bet: one learning principle, generalization,

32:12.950 --> 32:17.190
explains what we see, if not everywhere, then nearly enough that it doesn't matter

32:17.190 --> 32:24.490
for study. Studying networks directly works much better than most people expect.

32:24.490 --> 32:28.490
Understanding compounds as the map fills in. And maybe,

32:28.490 --> 32:31.004
just maybe, that's most of what we need.

32:35.004 --> 32:37.784
This isn't "neural networks are a bit cleaner than we thought".

32:37.784 --> 32:43.104
It's a different kind of explanation. Think of the move from intelligent design to

32:43.104 --> 32:47.684
natural selection: the point wasn't that a few more animals turned out to have

32:47.684 --> 32:51.084
evolved. It was realizing what process produced all of them.

32:51.084 --> 32:57.584
Here, that process is generalization. Nick puts the odds at maybe eighty-five

32:57.584 --> 32:58.384
percent.

32:58.118 --> 33:02.218
A tiger is orange for a reason: deer can't tell orange from green.

33:02.218 --> 33:05.278
Once you know that, a lot of the jungle makes sense.

33:05.278 --> 33:09.278
Neural networks are clean the way a rainforest is clean.

33:09.278 --> 33:15.298
Not tidy, but explainable. Nearly everything in it makes sense,

33:15.298 --> 33:17.139
once you know the process that made it.

33:17.589 --> 33:22.129
It also makes you humble about brains. Nick stared at the cat and car neuron,

33:22.129 --> 33:28.469
the V neuron, for years, with tools far better than any brain scientist has,

33:28.469 --> 33:32.309
and couldn't see what it was. If a neuron like that turned up in a human brain,

33:32.309 --> 33:38.529
seen through a few electrodes, Nick would give a "cats and cars" reading almost no

33:38.529 --> 33:42.742
weight. There'd be little chance of seeing what it really was.

33:43.192 --> 33:47.832
And for Nick, this isn't only about safety. A lot of it is wanting AI agents,

33:47.832 --> 33:53.172
one day, to understand who they really are, and themselves.

33:53.172 --> 33:56.652
As the essay puts it: they never asked to be created,

33:56.652 --> 33:58.046
and this is the least we can do.

33:59.246 --> 34:04.486
So, five things to take home. One: a neuron that seems to mean many things is

34:04.486 --> 34:09.646
usually one thing, or a few related things, we don't have a word for yet.

34:09.646 --> 34:15.686
Two: the famous evidence for superposition came from blurry tools and older designs,

34:15.686 --> 34:20.306
and it fell apart on a closer look. It should be the last resort,

34:20.306 --> 34:24.646
not the default. Three: networks group similar things together,

34:24.646 --> 34:29.409
and under pressure those groups get broader, not scrambled.

34:29.409 --> 34:35.566
Four: Nick's best guess is that broad explanations win more of the learning signal,

34:35.566 --> 34:40.626
so clean structure grows on its own, in image models and language models alike.

34:40.626 --> 34:43.766
And five: when something looks like a mess, treat it as a lead.

34:43.766 --> 34:48.106
With better maps, and enough automated scientists,

34:48.106 --> 34:49.210
the whole city might be read.

34:50.610 --> 34:54.421
Neural networks are already clean. We just have to learn how to read them.
